<%# THE HERO: heading, standfirst, actions and a picture of the product, composed as one block on the one
  dark field the page opens with.

  WHY DARK. The page used to open on paper and stay there: brand colour reached nothing but the buttons
  until the closing band 4,000px further down, and the product panel -- the only picture of the thing being
  sold -- was a white card on a white field, so it had no edge to separate it. On `lyn-ink` the panel is the
  brightest object on the screen without any decoration drawn around it, and the page gains the first beat of
  a rhythm it did not have: ink, then paper, then mist, then ink again at the close.

  THE EMPHASIS IS THE ONE HOT COLOUR ON THE PAGE. `highlight` is a phrase out of the heading that a
  translator may move to wherever their sentence actually turns. It was a magenta block on the original site;
  a redesign pass replaced it with a thin blue underline and `lyn-magenta` then appeared nowhere but a
  validation error, which is how the page lost its only accent. It is a magenta block again --
  docs/redesign/00-visual-baseline.md:52 named it a strength, and theme/colors.js:323 has already
  contrast-checked white type on it at 4.9:1. It is upright, not rotated: the tilt was the dated part, not
  the colour. `whitespace-nowrap` keeps the marked phrase from breaking across two lines, where one block
  would read as two unrelated marks.

  THE PANEL IS A PHOTOGRAPH OF THE PRODUCT, not a drawing of one -- see the note above the figure.
%>
<% primary = marketing_target_href(section['primary_target']) %>
<% secondary = marketing_target_href(section['secondary_target']) %>
<% heading = (page.heading.presence || page.title).to_s %>
<% highlight = section['highlight'].to_s %>
<% hero_shot = page&.image_for(section['attachment_id']) %>

<section class="relative isolate overflow-hidden bg-lyn-ink">
  <%# THE FIELD. Two soft pools of brand colour rather than a flat slab -- indigo behind the panel, royal
      low and wide behind the heading -- so the band has depth without anything drawn on it. They are
      `-z-10` inside this isolated section, and `overflow-hidden` is what keeps a pool from putting a
      horizontal scrollbar on a narrow screen. %>
  <span class="absolute -right-40 -top-56 -z-10 size-[46rem] rounded-full bg-[radial-gradient(circle,rgba(91,91,240,0.42),transparent_62%)]" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="absolute -bottom-72 -left-32 -z-10 size-[42rem] rounded-full bg-[radial-gradient(circle,rgba(29,63,168,0.38),transparent_64%)]" aria-hidden="true"></span>

  <%# THE SCREENSHOT RUNS OFF THE EDGE OF THE PAGE, and that is the point of this grid.
      The product shot is a 1440x900 capture of a four-column agent workspace. Sized to fit a tidy half of
      a 72rem container it renders about 460px wide, every label in it turns to grey mush, and the one
      picture of the product on the site says nothing about the product. Here the text column is fixed at
      30rem and the figure is given 68rem regardless of what is left, so it overruns its cell, crosses the
      container edge and is cut off by the viewport: about three quarters of the capture at three quarters
      of its own scale, which is the size at which a conversation list reads as a conversation list.

      A too-wide block overflows at its INLINE-END edge, so this works in both directions without a rule of
      its own -- rightwards in English, leftwards in Arabic, where the figure sits on the left. The section
      carries `overflow-hidden`, so the overrun is clipped at the viewport and cannot put a horizontal
      scrollbar on the document; that is checked at 390, 820 and 1440 by tools/visual/marketing-gate.mjs. %>
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl items-center gap-12 px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 lg:grid-cols-[31rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16 lg:py-28">
    <div class="motion-safe:animate-fade-in-up">
      <% if section['kicker'].present? %>
        <p class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-lyn-line-dark bg-lyn-ink/40 py-1.5 pe-4 ps-3 text-sm font-medium text-lyn-paper/80">
          <%# THE DOT IS ALIVE AGAIN. The ring is a second span that expands out of the dot and fades, so the
              dot keeps its box and the label beside it never moves. `motion-safe` on the ring alone: a
              reader who asked for stillness gets the plain dot, which is what the badge needs anyway. %>
          <span class="relative flex size-2 flex-none" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="absolute inset-0 rounded-full bg-lyn-blue motion-safe:animate-live-ring"></span>
            <span class="relative size-2 rounded-full bg-lyn-blue"></span>
          </span>
          <%= section['kicker'] %>
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <%# ONE H1, built from the row's own heading. The highlight is a literal phrase out of that string, so
          the two cannot disagree; a phrase that is not in the heading simply does not match and the heading
          renders whole. `split` on a literal needs no escaping of the needle, and every part is escaped by
          ERB on the way out.

          LEADING IS 1.16 AND NOT TIGHTER. This headline is set in IBM Plex Sans Arabic half the time, where
          the marks above and below the line need the room; display leading tuned on the Latin cut alone
          collides them. The highlight block needs a little more again, which is what `leading-[1.3]` and
          the negative inline margin on the block do: the lozenge is padded, and without the pull the padded
          line would sit wider than the unmarked ones. %>
      <h1 class="mt-6 text-balance text-[2.6rem] font-bold leading-[1.16] tracking-tight text-lyn-paper sm:text-[3rem] lg:text-[3.05rem]">
        <% if highlight.present? && heading.include?(highlight) %>
          <% before, after = heading.split(highlight, 2) %>
          <%= before %><span class="-mx-[0.12em] inline-block whitespace-nowrap rounded-lg bg-lyn-magenta px-[0.12em] py-[0.02em] text-lyn-paper"><%= highlight %></span><%= after %>
        <% else %>
          <%= heading %>
        <% end %>
      </h1>

      <% if page.lede.present? %>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-xl text-lg leading-[1.8] text-lyn-paper/70"><%= page.lede %></p>
      <% end %>

      <% if primary.present? || secondary.present? %>
        <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <% if primary.present? && section['primary_label'].present? %>
            <a href="<%= primary %>"
               class="inline-flex min-h-12 items-center rounded-xl bg-lyn-indigo px-7 text-base font-semibold text-lyn-paper shadow-[0_14px_38px_-12px_rgba(91,91,240,0.85)] transition-colors hover:bg-lyn-indigo-deep focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-lyn-blue">
              <%= section['primary_label'] %>
            </a>
          <% end %>
          <% if secondary.present? && section['secondary_label'].present? %>
            <a href="<%= secondary %>"
               class="inline-flex min-h-12 items-center rounded-xl border border-lyn-line-dark px-7 text-base font-semibold text-lyn-paper/90 transition-colors hover:border-lyn-blue hover:text-lyn-blue focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-lyn-blue">
              <%= section['secondary_label'] %>
            </a>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <%# THE PRODUCT, PHOTOGRAPHED -- not a drawing of it.

        WHAT WAS HERE BEFORE. A hand-built panel: a fake "Shared inbox" header, three invented customers
        with invented messages in `marketing.hero_panel.rows`, a fake composer and a fake "Assigned
        automatically" pill. It was the first thing every visitor saw and it was a picture of a product that
        does not look like that, with customers who do not exist. The owner's instruction is explicit -- a
        meaningful preview of the real product, not a fabricated dashboard -- and this is where that rule
        bites hardest, because the hero is the one image everybody sees.

        IF THERE IS NO SCREENSHOT, THERE IS NO PICTURE. No placeholder, no fallback to the old panel: a
        fallback that fabricates is still fabricating. The text column simply takes the full measure, which
        is a perfectly good hero, and `marketing:enrich` is how the capture gets attached.

        `rise-in` IS THE ONE ORCHESTRATED MOMENT on the page -- the element the page is about, settling in
        once. It respects reduced motion through `motion-safe`, so a reader who asked for stillness is given
        the screenshot already in place rather than an animation they cannot stop. %>
    <% if hero_shot.present? %>
      <div class="relative mx-auto w-full max-w-xl lg:mx-0 lg:w-[68rem] lg:max-w-none">
        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-2xl ring-1 ring-white/15 shadow-[0_44px_90px_-32px_rgba(0,0,0,0.75)] motion-safe:animate-rise-in">
          <img src="<%= marketing_media_path(id: hero_shot.id) %>"
               alt="<%= section['alt'].to_s %>"
               <%= "width=#{hero_shot.blob.metadata['width']} height=#{hero_shot.blob.metadata['height']}".html_safe if hero_shot.blob.metadata['width'].present? %>
               <%# THE ONE IMAGE ON THE SITE THAT IS NOT LAZY. It is the largest contentful paint: deferring
                   it is deferring the moment the page appears to have loaded. %>
               loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"
               class="block w-full">
        </figure>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>
